## Authorize FortiAP

On your lab FortiGate FGT-WLC within the HTTPS web GUI

-   Navigate to **Wi-Fi & Switch Controller \> Managed FortiAPs**

-   Identify the **FortiAP-231F & FortiAP-231G** access points which are
    connected to **ports 1,2, 3, and 4 and authorize these APs**.
    Authorize these by selecting the APs, selecting the Authorization
    drop-down button, and then selecting **Authorize**.

![](media/image28.png){width="6.5in"
height="2.0778346456692915in"}

![](media/image29.png){width="6.5in"
height="0.46944444444444444in"}

Once authorized, please wait a few minutes. The APs should come online
when they next attempt to discover the FortiGate (also known as Access
Controller or AC). Fortinet Access Points, by default, will attempt
multiple AC discovery methods, including FortiGate, FortiEdge Cloud, and
MESH. Depending on where these APs are in the discovery process, it may
result in a short delay before they appear in an online state.

[**Tip:** You can disable and re-enable power-over-ethernet (PoE) on
your FortiSwitch ports to speed up the discovery process. Fortinet APs
will always attempt to discover a FortiGate first as part of their AC
discovery cycle.]{.mark}

Once your lab APs are online, they should appear as shown in the image
below. Note: There's no need to wait for this process to complete;
please continue with the lab.

![A screenshot of a computer AI-generated content may be
incorrect.](media/image30.png){width="6.5in"
height="1.6958333333333333in"}

## Create Operational Profile for APs

As the APs are located within the Fortinet Wi-Fi lab in Sophia, France,
we must ensure that the AP radios are configured to operate within the
Wi-Fi regulatory requirements for France. Hence, you'll need to create
and assign an Operation Profile (aka wtp-profile) to the access points
within your pod.

### Create Operation Profile for FortiAP-231F

![A screenshot of a computer Description automatically
generated](media/image31.png){width="2.6215277777777777in"
height="1.4652777777777777in"}On your lab FortiGate FGT-WLC within the
HTTPS web GUI

-   Navigate to **WiFi & Switch Controller** \> **Operational Profiles**

-   In the top menu bar, ensure that the FortiAP Profiles option is
    selected

-   From the top menu bar, select the **+Create New** button

Within the **New FortiAP Profile** window, set the following:

-   Name: **FAP-231F-FR**

-   ![](media/image32.png){width="4.073611111111111in"
    height="2.5909722222222222in"}Platform: Select **FAP231F** from the
    dropdown list

-   Country/Region: Select the **Specify** option and choose
    **[France]{.mark}** from the dropdown list (as the default country
    is currently set to France, you can also use the default option)

Note: Alternatively, the default Wi-Fi country of operation can be set
on a system-wide basis under WiFi & Switch Controller \> WiFi Settings,
which will be applied to all existing or new FortiAP profiles. This can,
of course, be overridden if required, as per these same steps.

-   AP login password: **Set** to **fortinet**

-   Administrative Access: Enable **HTTPS** and **SSH**

Under Radio 1

-   ![](media/image33.png){width="1.8803149606299212in"
    height="0.6305938320209974in"}Set the mode to **Disabled** - Due to
    a high amount of RF congestion in the lab environment, you'll want
    to avoid using the 2.4GHz band for client device connectivity.

![](media/image34.png){width="4.175in"
height="4.108333333333333in"}Under Radio 2, set the following (some of
which may already be the default):

-   Radio Resource Provision: **Enable**

-   Band 5GHz**: 802.11ax/ac/n/a**

-   Channel Width: **20MHz**

-   Short Guard Interval (SGI): **Enabled --** Enabling this setting
    provides a 10% data-rate improvement, by setting a shorter (400ns)
    pause between frames as opposed to the default GI (800ns). Longer
    guard intervals are required for outdoor use cases where reflected
    RF frames can take a longer time to bounce back, creating a
    transmission overlap (interference).

-   Transmit Power Mode: **dBm 14 dBm**

-   SSIDs: **Bridge** -- For this workshop, you'll only be using Bridge
    mode SSIDs; hence, by setting this option, as soon as a bridge mode
    SSID is configured, it will be automatically provisioned to the AP
    radio.

Under Radio 3, set the following (some of which may already be the
default):

-   Mode: Dedicated Monitor

-   ![](media/image35.png){width="2.8979166666666667in"
    height="0.7138888888888889in"}WIDS profile: default

```{=html}
<!-- -->
```
-   Leave other settings as per default and select the **OK** button

### Create Operation Profile for FortiAP-231G

![A screenshot of a computer Description automatically
generated](media/image31.png){width="2.6215277777777777in"
height="1.4652777777777777in"}On your lab FortiGate FGT-WLC within the
HTTPS web GUI

-   Navigate to **WiFi & Switch Controller** \> **Operational Profiles**

-   In the top menu bar, ensure that the FortiAP Profiles option is
    selected

-   From the top menu bar, select the **+Create New** button

Within the **New FortiAP Profile** window, set the following:

-   Name: **FAP-231G-FR**

-   ![](media/image36.png){width="3.74375in"
    height="2.595138888888889in"}Platform: Select **FAP231G** from the
    dropdown list

-   Country/Region: Select the **Specify** option and choose
    **[France]{.mark}** from the dropdown list (as the default country
    is currently set to France, you can also use the default option)

Note: Alternatively, the default Wi-Fi country of operation can be set
on a system-wide basis under WiFi & Switch Controller \> WiFi Settings,
which will be applied to all existing or new FortiAP profiles. This can,
of course, be overridden if required, as per these same steps.

-   AP login password: **Set** to **fortinet**

-   Administrative Access: Enable **HTTPS** and **SSH**

Under Radio 1

-   Set the mode to **Dedicated Monitor** -- This will serve only to
    monitor the RF for any WIDS/Rogue APs. Since this is deployed within
    a lab environment, there is significant congestion on the 2.4GHz
    band. Hence, it's not suitable to operate as a service radio for
    client devices to connect to.

![](media/image37.png){width="2.9599300087489064in"
height="0.6305938320209974in"}

WIDS profile: Set to **enable** and select the **default** profile from
within the drop-down menu.

Under Radio 2, set the following (some of which may already be the
default):

-   ![](media/image38.png){width="4.699305555555555in"
    height="4.215277777777778in"}Radio Resource Provision: **Enable**

-   WIDS profile: **Enable** select **default**

-   Band 5GHz**: 802.11ax/ac/n/a**

-   Channel Width: **20MHz**

-   Short Guard Interval (SGI): **Enabled --** Enabling this setting
    provides a 10% data-rate improvement, by setting a shorter (400ns)
    pause between frames as opposed to the default GI (800ns). Longer
    guard intervals are required for outdoor use cases where reflected
    RF frames can take a longer time to bounce back (echo), creating a
    transmission overlap (interference).

-   Transmit Power Mode: **dBm 14 dBm**

-   SSIDs: **Bridge** -- For this workshop, you'll only be using Bridge
    mode SSIDs; hence, by setting this option, as soon as a bridge mode
    SSID is configured, it will be automatically provisioned to the AP
    radio.

Under Radio 3, set the following (some of which may already be the
default);

![](media/image39.png){width="4.718055555555556in"
height="3.779166666666667in"}

-   WIDS profile: **Enable** select **default**

-   Radio Resource Provision: **Enable**

-   Band 6GHz**: 802.11ax**

-   Channel Width: **80MHz** -- 80MHz should be the minimum channel
    width selected for 6GHz band, unless the same SSID is advertised on
    the 5GHz band. 20MHz and 40MHz 6GHz Wi-Fi channels that are not
    operating on a preferred scanning channel (PSC) do not beacon;
    hence, the only way a client will know a 6GHz SSID is provisioned is
    through reduced neighbour reports (RNRs) on the 5GHz SSID.

-   Short Guard Interval: **Enabled**

-   Transmit Power Mode: **dBm 17 dBm (as a best practice,** always try
    to balance the radio power between 2.4GHz/5GHz/6GHz radios. In this
    case, by setting the power to 17 dBm, the TXP is nearly 3 dB higher
    (a doubling of power). This can help ensure that a client device
    prefers to connect to the 6 GHz band over the 5 GHz band, as the
    RSSI should appear higher for the 6 GHz band.

-   SSIDs: **Bridge**

-   Leave other settings as per default and select the **OK** button

