## Configure NTP on FGT-WLC

Continuing from above. Configuring Network Time Protocol (NTP) is highly
recommended, as several key components in this Hands-on Lab rely on the
correct time, such as certificate issuance, certificate expiry, and
logging timestamps.

The goal of this lab section is to ensure that your FGT-WLC and
FortiAIOps-VM appliances are configured to operate in the correct time
zone and are obtaining their time from an online NTP service provider,
such as FortiGuard.

-   If you haven't already, please open a HTTPS browser session to your
    FGT-WLC appliance, <https://10.222.14.XX:14001>, log in using your
    admin credentials, and perform the following steps.

```{=html}
<!-- -->
```
-   Navigate to **System \> Settings**

-   Set the Time zone: **GMT+1 Paris, etc. in EMEA,** or **GMT+8 Kuala
    Lumpur, etc. in APAC**, etc

-   ![A screenshot of a computer Description automatically
    generated](media/image45.png){width="2.8819444444444446in"
    height="1.8333333333333333in"}Select server: Ensure **FortiGuard**
    is selected

-   Under **Listen on Interfaces:** Add **AP-Mgmt, Client-VLAN-A, and
    Client-VLAN-B**

-   Select the **Apply** button

# Lab Section 2: Configure Domain Name Services

Domain name services (DNS) are required as part of this workshop, as
devices will need to be able to resolve fully qualified domain names to
IP addresses. We will be using SSL PKI issued certificates (which can
only be tied to a qualified domain name) for components within the
workshop, specifically the FortiGateWLC-VM and FortiAIOps-VM appliances.

The goal of section 3 is to configure DNS and ensure we have host (A)
records for the FGT-WLC and FortiAIOps appliances. DNS is required in
any production deployment. However, you don't need to host DNS Services
on the Fortigate; you could use any third-party DNS Server, which is
more commonly the case in production deployments.

