### Performance \> AP Health

![](media/image148.png){width="4.7in"
height="3.7583333333333333in"}The Diagnostic and Tools pop-out window
should appear, displaying details related to the specifically selected
Access Point, with the Performance \> AP Health page initially
displayed.

Within this page, you'll notice many key wireless performance
statistics, many of which can highlight if there are potential Wi-Fi
user experience issues, such as;

-   AP CPU Usage

-   Channel Utilization

-   Number of Clients

#### Performance \> Wireless

-   From the tabs listed, under **Performance,** select the **Wireless**
    tab

![](media/image149.png){width="6.5in"
height="0.5666666666666667in"}

You should now see further Wi-Fi performance metrics which can and are
used to highlight Wi-Fi user experience issues such as;

**Transmission: Discards (loss) and Retries;** If seen, this will often
mean users will be experiencing a degradation in performance

![](media/image150.png){width="5.258333333333334in"
height="3.7118055555555554in"}**Noise:** Noise (background RF
interference) can be detrimental if it is too high, directly impacting
the client\'s signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) or their ability to hear and
decode wireless frames. This can result in clients needing to drop to
lower data rates to transmit or receive, or if too high, can result in
excessive retries and loss occurring. Ideally, you'd want the noise in
your environment to be lower than -90 dBm, but this may be impossible,
especially on the 2.4 GHz band, which is becoming more and more
saturated with wearables, like EarPods, Watches, Rings, and other IoT
devices.

### Spectrum Analysis

All currently supported FortiGate Managed APs can function as a Wi-Fi
spectrum analyzer on the bands for which their radios are capable of
operating.

This can help identify wireless user experience issues caused by
non-Wi-Fi interference, which is becoming increasingly common,
especially in the green energy and energy-saving sectors.

More entities are transitioning to energy-star-rated offerings, such as
smart homes and smart buildings, to reduce their energy costs.
Structures with higher energy-star ratings often have mandatory
energy-saving devices that can and do interfere with wireless networks.

Many customers\' Wi-Fi networks, including several Fortinet offices
worldwide, have fallen victim to energy-saving devices that have
directly impacted wireless deployments.

The most common cause of these issues is the use of motion sensors,
which can activate and deactivate lighting.
[Example](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/xtb3trmy3cmj5dp5bzgud/DET150.pdf?rlkey=1d9atkqj40wgrq83mtlsw8p5k&dl=0)

![](media/image151.jpeg){width="6.147222222222222in"
height="4.55in"}These devices typically operate within the UNII-3 band,
primarily around 5.8 GHz, but also cross into the 5.7 GHz and 5.9 GHz
ranges. Hence, this means typically that CH149-165 are unusable where
these devices have been deployed. The image below contains an example of
what you may see if using an Ekahau Sidekick as a spectrum analyzer in
the same space.

Now, if you have a Spectrum Analyzer available, such as an Ekahau Side
Kick, you would see something similar to the image below, highlighting a
continuous transmitter nearby.

However, as most customers don't have high-end Wi-Fi troubleshooting
hardware on hand, using the Spectrum Analyzer capability built into
FortiAP/FortiAIOps can also help identify if there's an RF problem.

Note: The signal interference doesn't need to be relatively loud, just
persistent. That's all it takes. However, in your lab environment, it's
very easy to see why the 2.4GHz band isn't ideal to use.

![](media/image152.png){width="6.5in"
height="1.5979166666666667in"}

Continuing within the AP Diagnostics and Tools window

-   ![](media/image153.png){width="2.050177165354331in"
    height="1.5834700349956254in"}Navigate to **Spectrum Analysis**

> Within the Spectrum Analysis Dashboard, select the following:

-   Band: **2.4GHz**

-   Radio: **Radio 3**

-   Channels: **1-13**

-   X-Axis: **MHz or Channels** (whatever you prefer)

-   Then select the **Start** button

It will take some time for the Spectrum analysis data to be shown. Let
it run for approximately 1- 2 minutes and then look at the results. It
should start to look like the images below;

![](media/image154.png){width="3.845138888888889in"
height="2.316666666666667in"}As you can see from the Signal Interference
Spectrograms, the 2.4GHz channel is showing significant RF noise.

![](media/image155.png){width="3.425in"
height="2.2805555555555554in"}By further comparing this to the Duty
Cycle Spectrogram, we can see that all 2.4 GHz channels, especially
channels 1 and 6 in this case, are nearly 100% utilized. If any Wi-Fi
users were connected to the 2.4GHz band on these channels in this space,
they would indeed face an inferior user experience.

**Question:** If you had to select a 2.4GHz channel in this environment,
what channel would you use?

![](media/image156.png){width="6.491666666666666in"
height="1.3666666666666667in"}If the non-Wi-Fi signal matches a known
reference pattern, the type of device causing the interference will also
be listed, which can help to locate the source.

**Please note:** A FortiAP\'s primary task is to code and decode 802.11
(Wi-Fi) frames, not to perform spectrum analysis. If you are after a
dedicated spectrum analyzer, do not obtain a FortiAP for this purpose.
There are far superior tools available, such as the Ekahau SideKick or
Oscium Wi-Spy. However, based on the results from your lab, you can see
that it can be a valuable tool for isolating a potential root cause of
Wi-Fi user experience and connectivity-related issues.

### Channel Summary Dashboard

The channel summary dashboards are unique to FortiAIOps and provide some
of the best insights into any monitored RF environment.

The Channel Distribution dashboard lets you visualize the channel
deployment and re-use across your entire Fortinet Wi-Fi deployment.

This dashboard is essential if you are implementing a static channel
plan and want to ensure the optimal use and reuse of available channels,
while also confirming that the channel widths set per Access Point (AP)
are as per Wi-Fi design, i.e., 20MHz, 40MHz, or 80MHz.

-   Within FortiAIOps, navigate to **Wireless \> Channel Summary**

You should see the Channel Distribution Dashboard as shown below

As you have 4x AP radios running 5GHz in your lab(2 x FAP-231F and 2x
FAP-231G). You should see 4 x 20MHz channels in use as per the image
below.

You should also see 2 x 80MHz channels in use for the 2 x AP radios
running 6GHz (2x FAP-231G) in your lab as per the image below.

![](media/image157.png){width="6.8597222222222225in"
height="4.05in"}

-   Within the Wireless \> Channel Summary Dashboard, **navigate to the
    Power Distribution Dashboard** by selecting the **Power
    Distribution** option on the upper right side of the window.

This dashboard allows you to view the radio TX power distribution across
all the selected AP radios in your environment.

![](media/image158.png){width="6.308333333333334in"
height="3.5868055555555554in"}You should see 4x AP radios operating
\@14dBm (4 x 5GHz) and 2 x radios operating \@17dBm (2x 6GHz)

Within either dashboard, you can select and drill down to confirm which
APs are aligned to the data metrics being shown.

Whilst you won't cover this specifically, you can also view this data
using the filter tool in the top corner to restrict this view to a
per-site/building/floor basis if needed. Alternatively, you can also use
Device Group to limit the view to a per FortiGate basis, if necessary.
This does require that the sites, buildings, and floors be defined
within the Wireless \> Heatmaps Section beforehand.

![](media/image159.png){width="3.421709317585302in"
height="1.2939227909011373in"}

### Rogue APs

Within FortiAIOps, navigate to **Wireless \> Rogue APs**

![](media/image160.png){width="6.483333333333333in"
height="2.533333333333333in"}

-   If a rogue AP is shown as online, this means it's currently
    broadcasting within the vicinity of your APs

-   If the Signal Interference, i.e., RSSI of the Rogue, is \> 75 dBm,
    it will likely be within 10m of your APs.

-   If detected by 3 or more APs, you should be able to locate the
    device within your environment. FortiAIOps will list the 3 APs that
    can hear the rogue at the highest RSSI.

-   If the device is seen Off-wire, that means it's not connected or
    seen on the wired side of the network. This identification method
    uses the AP base MAC address. If the Ethernet MAC is visible on the
    wired side and aligns with the radio MAC on the wireless side, it
    would be flagged as on-wire.

-   What's a Rogue in the Wi-Fi sense? If you believe it's a malicious
    Wi-Fi device, sorry, you're misinformed.

-   Even Google AI provides the misinformed answer, i.e., you can search
    What is a Rogue access point and get this bogus response: "A rogue
    access point is **a wireless access point that has been installed on
    a secure network** without explicit authorization from a local
    network administrator." Yes, this does fall into the definition of a
    rogue, but it's not entirely correct.

-   A rogue is **any interfering Wi-Fi-enabled device seen by your
    managed access points;** this could be a station or access point.
    This can be as simple as a neighbouring AP that's not part of your
    managed network, and nothing more than that. It doesn't need to be
    malicious or connected to your network to be considered a rogue.

-   Rogue APs and clients can harm Wi-Fi performance. However, 802.11,
    being a contention-based standard, does a great job of sharing the
    airtime with neighboring devices on the same channels (so long as
    they can hear each other).

-   Meaning two or more APs, on the same channel and channel width, will
    indeed share the channel airtime as equally as possible. Where
    things can and often do go wrong is when an Access Point (AP) is
    configured to operate using 40MHz or 80MHz wide channels, while at
    the same time the neighbouring APs are set to run on 20MHz wide
    channels, resulting in overlapping Basic Service Set (BSS) issues,
    also known as Wi-Fi death for the APs running on the wider channels.
    (No, this is not going to be a lesson around OBSS).

-   However, it's highly recommended to identify rogue access points
    and, where possible, configure your Wi-Fi network to a point where
    these rogue APs will have as minimal impact. FortiAIOps enables this
    by displaying all current rogue APs detected by all managed APs
    across all FortiGates from a single central location.

-   When combined with the Wireless Heatmaps and Wireless Location
    Services engine available within FortiAIOps, you can also
    triangulate and see where rogue devices are in your environment to
    remove them should it be necessary.

## Switching Dashboards

### FortSwitches

-   Within FortiAIOps, navigate to **Switch \> FortiSwitches**

 This page displays a graphical snapshot of the FortiSwitch activity,
such as; the total number of FortiSwitches, their status
(online/offline/unauthorized), and the deployed model details

From here, you can drill down into your managed FortiSwitches to see
further statistics and diagnostics in detail.

![](media/image161.png){width="6.5in"
height="1.2451662292213472in"}

-   **Select** your lab FortiSwitch i.e. **LABX-224EPOE-1** ß where X is
    your Pod#

-   Select the **View details** button

![](media/image162.png){width="6.5in"
height="3.6381944444444443in"}The Diagnostics and Tools pop-out window
should appear, which includes the current FortiSwitch face plate/port
usage, hardware summary, general status, statistics, and specific
configuration details.

There are several other tabs available: Cable Test, Logs, Statistics,
and Clients.

#### Cable Test

The Cable test is a diagnostic and troubleshooting tool that allows you
to check the state of cable pairs between the FortiSwitch and the
devices connected to its physical copper ports. This tool does not work
on fiber ports and may provide some inaccuracy for very short (\<1m) or
very long cable runs (\>100m).

It's a straightforward tool to use; you need to select an active port
and then select the Diagnose button.

![](media/image163.png){width="6.445935039370079in"
height="1.36875in"}

Unfortunately, the cable test doesn't work on the FortiSwitch-224E-POE
switches in the lab. It should, but doesn't. **Please don't run this,**
as it can result in the switch port going into an offline state. The
switch will need to be rebooted to restore port functionality if a Cable
Test is run.

#### Logs

Under the Logs tab, a![](media/image164.png){width="6.5in"
height="1.0847222222222221in"}ll FortiSwitch logs are available for up
to 1 week.

### Statistics

Within the Statistics tab, you can view all SwitchPort Ethernet frame
metrics. This can be used to identify connectivity and performance
issues on Ethernet links. For example, if you are seeing TX, RX, or CRC
Errors, this could indicate a cabling or switchport speed negotiation
issue. This data is available for up to 24 months, depending on storage
and data availability.

You can also drill down into each port to view these stats over time.

### Wired Clients

-   Within FortiAIOps, navigate to **Switch \> Wired Clients**

From this Dashboard, you can monitor the wired clients seen by the
FortiSwitches that are deployed in your network. This page displays a
graphical snapshot of client activity, including the total number of
FortiSwitch clients, their status (online or offline), client device
details, and associated VLANs. Hovering over the charts provides
specific statistics, and clicking on a specific area on the chart
filters the data displayed on this page.

![](media/image165.png){width="6.5in"
height="2.073611111111111in"}

A great use case for this dashboard is identifying which switchport a
client is connected to on the network at a specific point in time.
Recently, a customer in Australia used this dashboard to locate a
malicious botnet client in their network. The MAC address appeared to be
spoofed, and all they had was the device name to go from. Without
FortiAIOps, they don't believe they could have isolated the client as
quickly as they did.

## SD-WAN Dashboards

The SD-WAN Dashboards provide insights into the monitored SD-WAN
interfaces. Using machine learning and AI modelling, this dashboard will
show when an interface is or has been impacted and predict when further
impacts may occur.

The SD-WAN dashboard has been significantly improved within FortiAIOps
3.0; Improvements include

-   Monitoring/Dashboard improvements

-   AI-Driven Predictive Modelling and Anomaly Detection (Forecasting)

-   Dynamic Baselining Model of Normal Network Behaviour (Dynamic SLA
    baseline)

-   Deviation Analysis for Proactive Anomaly Detection

-   Many new SLAs to improve WAN alerting and performance, with the
    ultimate goal of improving WAN reliability and user experience.

### Insights Dashboard

-   ![](media/image166.png){width="2.1527777777777777in"
    height="0.3138888888888889in"}Within FortiAIOps, navigate to
    **SD-WAN \> Insights**

-   In the top menu bar, under FortiGate, ensure your lab **FGT-WLC** is
    selected and that your SD-WAN interface **port5** is selected

-   Then select the **Apply** button

The Insight Dashboard displays current and historical SD-WAN performance
metrics, which can be traced back 24 months, provided the data is
available within FortiOps. This can be used to examine SD-WAN link
performance and utilization statistics over time. For example, to
determine if and when more bandwidth may be needed.

![](media/image167.png){width="6.461298118985127in"
height="3.255207786526684in"}

**Note:** To obtain all the metrics shown in this dashboard, all SD-WAN
configurations must be in place as per Lab Section 9, along with
Application monitoring and traffic logging being enabled on the relevant
outbound firewall policies.

Several of these widgets, such as Performance Status, can also be
drilled down into to gain further insights related to the displayed
data, including reviewing individual performance results for each SD-WAN
SLA, as shown in the image below.

![](media/image168.png){width="6.5in"
height="0.9180555555555555in"}

Looking at the SD-WAN Insights dashboard, you will also notice the MOS
Score or Mean Optimisation Score. A MOS is a numerical measure of the
human-judged overall quality of an event or experience. In SD-WAN, a
Mean Opinion Score is a ranking of the quality of voice and video
sessions.

MOS is judged on a scale of 1 (bad) to 5 (excellent), and is the average
of a number of what would typically be human-scored individual
parameters. Within FortiAIOps, the MOS is generated by an Objective
Measurement Method that approximates a human ranking based on
performance metrics observed over time. For this to be accurate, your
SD-WAN baselines must be accurate. Therefore, it's essential to check
your SD-WAN performance monitors and ensure the threshold values set for
the following are at a level that should always be within the SLA,
unless there is a real performance issue.

-   Latency Threshold

-   Jitter Threshold

-   Packet Loss Threshold

### Forecasting Dashboard

-   ![](media/image169.png){width="3.1506944444444445in"
    height="0.37222222222222223in"}Within FortiAIOps, navigate to
    **SD-WAN \> Forecasting**

-   In the top menu bar, under FortiGate, ensure your lab **FGT-WLC** is
    selected, that one of your Health Checks is selected, i.e., **Ping
    SLA to MS Teams**, and that **port5** is selected

The Forecasting Dashboard requires multiple days, i.e., a week or more,
of data points (observations) to start providing predictive modeling.
Hence, it will not be possible for you to utilize the capabilities of
this dashboard fully within your Hands-On-Lab. However, below are some
images from an AIOps instance that has been operational for several
weeks.

These dashboards show current and historical latency, jitter, and packet
loss statistics for all monitored SD-WAN links, for up to 24 months,
depending on data availability

From the previous week\'s SD-WAN dashboard data below, you will notice
the following;

-   Latency, Jitter, and Packet loss (performance) stats

-   How often each of the defined SLAs were breached (Anomalies)

-   How often the link was inside and outside of SLA boundaries

-   Any outages that may have occurred, i.e., **SLA down**

-   Most common issue resulting in SD-WAN SLA breaches

![](media/image170.png){width="6.5in"
height="4.056603237095363in"}

Comparing last week\'s data to the current week, you will notice;

-   Forecasting expectations of the latency, jitter, and packet loss.

-   Current performance stats

![](media/image171.png){width="6.5in"
height="4.024926727909011in"}

![](media/image172.png){width="3.421527777777778in"
height="2.7555555555555555in"}Note: You can also drill down into each
datapoint for further analysis and insights as to what caused and
flagged anomalies (SLA breach). *Please note: This drill down may not
